Adultery","title":"Adultery"},{"id":"ArchaeologyandtheBible","title":"Archaeology and the Bible*"},{"id":"Birthright","title":"Birthright"},{"id":"Bloodguilt","title":"Bloodguilt*"},{"id":"ChastenChastisement","title":"Chasten, Chastisement*"},{"id":"ConcubinageConcubines","title":"Concubinage*, Concubines"},{"id":"CourtsandTrials","title":"Courts and Trials"},{"id":"Covenant","title":"Covenant"},{"id":"CriminalLawandPunishment","title":"Criminal Law and Punishment*"},{"id":"Crucifixion","title":"Crucifixion"},{"id":"CurseCursed","title":"Curse, Cursed"},{"id":"DeuteronomyBookof","title":"Deuteronomy, Book of"},{"id":"Discipline","title":"Discipline"},{"id":"Elder","title":"Elder"},{"id":"Elijah","title":"Elijah"},{"id":"Elisha","title":"Elisha"},{"id":"FamilyLifeandRelations","title":"Family Life and Relations"},{"id":"Forgiveness","title":"Forgiveness"},{"id":"FuneralCustoms","title":"Funeral Customs"},{"id":"Heir","title":"Heir"},{"id":"HoseaBookof","title":"Hosea, Book of"},{"id":"Impalement","title":"Impalement"},{"id":"Inheritance","title":"Inheritance"},{"id":"Isaac","title":"Isaac"},{"id":"LawBiblicalConceptof","title":"Law, Biblical Concept of"},{"id":"MarriageMarriageCustoms","title":"Marriage, Marriage Customs"},{"id":"Messiah","title":"Messiah"},{"id":"Obedience","title":"Obedience"},{"id":"Primogeniture","title":"Primogeniture*"},{"id":"Ransom","title":"Ransom"}],"dictionaryWords":[{"evidence":[{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"firstborn","senseEvidence":{"basis":"source_verse_citation","originalCitations":[{"anchorIndex":28,"href":"?bref=Deut.21.17","rawReference":"Deut.21.17"}],"ruleId":"Firstborn::Firstborn::source_verse_citation","sourceHeadwordId":"Firstborn"},"text":"Instead, he must acknowledge the firstborn, the son of his unloved wife, by giving him a double portion of all that he has. For that son is the firstfruits of his father’s strength; the right of the firstborn belongs to him.","verse":17}],"id":"Firstborn","label":"firstborn","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Firstborn::Firstborn::source_verse_citation","scope":"source_verse_citation","senseReview":"Reviewed original headword and source sense. This substantive word is admitted only where the original article explicitly cites the exact occurrence verse; ambiguous homographs and known context mismatches are separately excluded. This is selective contextual evidence, not an automatic rule for every same-spelling word.","sourceHeadwordId":"Firstborn","term":"Firstborn"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Firstborn","sourceTitle":"Firstborn"},{"evidence":[{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"If one is found slain, lying in a field in the land that the LORD your God is giving you to possess, and it is not known who killed him,","verse":1},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"And the priests, the sons of Levi, shall come forward, for the LORD your God has chosen them to serve Him and pronounce blessings in His name and to give a ruling in every dispute and case of assault.","verse":5},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"Accept this atonement, O LORD, for Your people Israel whom You have redeemed, and do not hold the shedding of innocent blood against them.” And the bloodshed will be atoned for.","verse":8},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"So you shall purge from among you the guilt of shedding innocent blood, since you have done what is right in the eyes of the LORD.","verse":9},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"When you go to war against your enemies and the LORD your God delivers them into your hand and you take them captive,","verse":10},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"you must not leave the body on the tree overnight, but you must be sure to bury him that day, because anyone who is hung on a tree is under God’s curse. You must not defile the land that the LORD your God is giving you as an inheritance.","verse":23}],"id":"Lord","label":"LORD","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","scope":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","senseReview":"Only literal uppercase LORD is admitted globally. Source Lord article explicitly covers God as Lord; lowercase human lord and sentence-title Lord are not inferred from this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord","term":"LORD"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Lord","sourceTitle":"Lord"}],"format":"bsb-study-chapter-v2","notes":[{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Deut.21.1-9","r":"Deut.21.1-9","rawR":"Deut.21.1-9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.21.1-9","value":"Deut.21.1-9"}],"t":"ref","v":"21:1-9"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The guilt of murder was usually removed through the death of the murderer (",{"href":"?bref=Deut.19.11-13","r":"Deut.19.11-13","rawR":"Deut.19.11-13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.19.11-13","value":"Deut.19.11-13"}],"t":"ref","v":"19:11-13"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Num.35.30-34","r":"Num.35.30-34","rawR":"Num.35.30-34","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.35.30-34","value":"Num.35.30-34"}],"t":"ref","v":"Num 35:30-34"},"). However, if the murderer was unknown, the guilt could still be removed by using this ritual."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Deut.21.1-9","refs":[{"book":"DEU","endChapter":21,"endVerse":9,"startChapter":21,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Deut.21.1-9","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"DEU","sourceChapterCoverage":[21],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"9631ee8e1ca408f5099171ad23ae8429b1414073ebdadced38c4fa1691992a2c","sourceItemName":"Deut.21.1-9","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":2283,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"DEU","canonicalOSIS":"Deut","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Deut"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Deut.21.1-9","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Deut.21.1-9","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Deut.21.1","r":"Deut.21.1","rawR":"Deut.21.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.21.1","value":"Deut.21.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"21:1"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," In contrast to the word translated ",{"c":["murder"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," in the Ten Commandments (see ",{"href":"?item=Deut.5.17_StudyNote_Filament","id":"Deut.5.17_StudyNote_Filament","rawId":"Deut.5.17_StudyNote_Filament","t":"link","v":"study note on 5:17"},"), the Hebrew verb here translated ",{"c":["murdered"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (literally ",{"c":["pierced through"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"},") indicates clear evidence that the death was not by natural causes."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Deut.21.1","refs":[{"book":"DEU","endChapter":21,"endVerse":1,"startChapter":21,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Deut.21.1","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"DEU","sourceChapterCoverage":[21],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"dc5a1c31730840c9caf16073ebf229e2ebe3bfbbf44fa3e6ac742414cf7d5804","sourceItemName":"Deut.21.1","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":2284,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"DEU","canonicalOSIS":"Deut","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Deut"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Deut.21.1","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Deut.21.1","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Deut.21.3-8","r":"Deut.21.3-8","rawR":"Deut.21.3-8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.21.3-8","value":"Deut.21.3-8"}],"t":"ref","v":"21:3-8"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," An avenger from the victim’s family (",{"href":"?bref=Deut.19.6-7","r":"Deut.19.6-7","rawR":"Deut.19.6-7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.19.6-7","value":"Deut.19.6-7"}],"t":"ref","v":"19:6-7"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Num.35.16-29","r":"Num.35.16-29","rawR":"Num.35.16-29","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.35.16-29","value":"Num.35.16-29"}],"t":"ref","v":"Num 35:16-29"},") could not hold the whole community responsible."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Deut.21.3-8","refs":[{"book":"DEU","endChapter":21,"endVerse":8,"startChapter":21,"startVerse":3}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Deut.21.3-8","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"DEU","sourceChapterCoverage":[21],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"f776aa4aeb2f4440ff763397e57b18a80511d35b12fb7903b27f5668dd5f31c1","sourceItemName":"Deut.21.3-8","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":2285,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"DEU","canonicalOSIS":"Deut","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Deut"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Deut.21.3-8","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Deut.21.3-8","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Deut.21.4","r":"Deut.21.4","rawR":"Deut.21.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.21.4","value":"Deut.21.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"21:4"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The ",{"c":["stream"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," would be an ever-flowing (perennial) brook located in an isolated area, free of contaminants."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Deut.21.4","refs":[{"book":"DEU","endChapter":21,"endVerse":4,"startChapter":21,"startVerse":4}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Deut.21.4","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"DEU","sourceChapterCoverage":[21],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"c08ee5b8a9c70999aa858546d065496ee2032283aeb70a482cee7a91e96a27ea","sourceItemName":"Deut.21.4","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":2286,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"DEU","canonicalOSIS":"Deut","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Deut"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Deut.21.4","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Deut.21.4","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Deut.21.6","r":"Deut.21.6","rawR":"Deut.21.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.21.6","value":"Deut.21.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"21:6"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["wash their hands:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," This ancient practice asserted innocence (cp. ",{"href":"?bref=Matt.27.24","r":"Matt.27.24","rawR":"Matt.27.24","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Matt.27.24","value":"Matt.27.24"}],"t":"ref","v":"Matt 27:24"},"); by it the community would declare that it was not responsible for a crime committed in its vicinity."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Deut.21.6","refs":[{"book":"DEU","endChapter":21,"endVerse":6,"startChapter":21,"startVerse":6}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Deut.21.6","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"DEU","sourceChapterCoverage":[21],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"d5c143336cc9d42e9ab571c4acbdbbf9196e5b4b92dfd0a380dd7d7f81a18fd0","sourceItemName":"Deut.21.6","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":2287,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"DEU","canonicalOSIS":"Deut","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Deut"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Deut.21.6","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Deut.21.6","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Deut.21.12","r":"Deut.21.12","rawR":"Deut.21.12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.21.12","value":"Deut.21.12"}],"t":"ref","v":"21:12"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," To ",{"c":["shave her head"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," and ",{"c":["cut her nails"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," demonstrated a captive woman’s separation from her home and family. She was cutting off the past to join a new family and community."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Deut.21.12","refs":[{"book":"DEU","endChapter":21,"endVerse":12,"startChapter":21,"startVerse":12}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Deut.21.12","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"DEU","sourceChapterCoverage":[21],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"07c6e807ca720d2e1f9b922d4c50d4a81590b4875b0b73b667567d813e104c37","sourceItemName":"Deut.21.12","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":2288,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"DEU","canonicalOSIS":"Deut","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Deut"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Deut.21.12","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Deut.21.12","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Deut.21.13","r":"Deut.21.13","rawR":"Deut.21.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.21.13","value":"Deut.21.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"21:13"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," To ",{"c":["change the clothes she was wearing"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," symbolically communicated taking on a new life and identifying with a new family."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Deut.21.13","refs":[{"book":"DEU","endChapter":21,"endVerse":13,"startChapter":21,"startVerse":13}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Deut.21.13","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"DEU","sourceChapterCoverage":[21],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"b7ea318eafcaee03286b557e106826ffca70b771bed1ff81a136aefa34bcbdff","sourceItemName":"Deut.21.13","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":2289,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"DEU","canonicalOSIS":"Deut","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Deut"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Deut.21.13","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Deut.21.13","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Deut.21.14","r":"Deut.21.14","rawR":"Deut.21.14","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.21.14","value":"Deut.21.14"}],"t":"ref","v":"21:14"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["humiliated her:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," By first taking her prisoner and then divorcing her, a captive woman’s husband devalued her. To protect her from further disgrace, the law prohibited him from selling her as a slave or from using her as one. Instead, she must be allowed to ",{"c":["go free"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},"."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Deut.21.14","refs":[{"book":"DEU","endChapter":21,"endVerse":14,"startChapter":21,"startVerse":14}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Deut.21.14","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"DEU","sourceChapterCoverage":[21],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"afdb471f2f3b8a5d465f29f1ee761c4597ec44c6772a66d2a92e2866349a1a37","sourceItemName":"Deut.21.14","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":2290,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"DEU","canonicalOSIS":"Deut","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Deut"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Deut.21.14","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Deut.21.14","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Deut.21.15-17","r":"Deut.21.15-17","rawR":"Deut.21.15-17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.21.15-17","value":"Deut.21.15-17"}],"t":"ref","v":"21:15-17"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The law required that a ",{"c":["firstborn son"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," receive the greater share of his father’s inheritance (see ",{"href":"?bref=Deut.21.15-16","r":"Deut.21.15-16","rawR":"Deut.21.15-16","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.21.15-16","value":"Deut.21.15-16"}],"t":"ref","v":"21:15-16"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.2.9","r":"2Kgs.2.9","rawR":"2Kgs.2.9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.2.9","value":"2Kgs.2.9"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Kgs 2:9"},"). The custom of giving the firstborn son a ",{"c":["double portion"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," is first recorded here but was implied in earlier practice (see ",{"href":"?bref=Gen.25.31-34","r":"Gen.25.31-34","rawR":"Gen.25.31-34","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.25.31-34","value":"Gen.25.31-34"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gen 25:31-34"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Gen.27.1-4","r":"Gen.27.1-4","rawR":"Gen.27.1-4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.27.1-4","value":"Gen.27.1-4"}],"t":"ref","v":"27:1-4"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Gen.48.8-22","r":"Gen.48.8-22","rawR":"Gen.48.8-22","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.48.8-22","value":"Gen.48.8-22"}],"t":"ref","v":"48:8-22"},"). • ",{"c":["son of his father’s virility:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," The eldest male child carried this distinction because he proved that the man was capable of siring children, and a boy in particular."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Deut.21.15-17","refs":[{"book":"DEU","endChapter":21,"endVerse":17,"startChapter":21,"startVerse":15}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Deut.21.15-17","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"DEU","sourceChapterCoverage":[21],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"e51a35fd6690617ccab74d7028a377b3b926b3f57734a4c4d1d7702356449e29","sourceItemName":"Deut.21.15-17","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":2291,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"DEU","canonicalOSIS":"Deut","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Deut"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Deut.21.15-17","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Deut.21.15-17","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Deut.21.20","r":"Deut.21.20","rawR":"Deut.21.20","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.21.20","value":"Deut.21.20"}],"t":"ref","v":"21:20"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," Calling someone ",{"c":["a glutton and a drunkard"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," was probably a proverbial cliché suggesting self-indulgence and laziness. Such a son was a good-for-nothing who rebelled against his parents and thus also against the community and divine authority (see ",{"href":"?bref=Pr.23.20-21","r":"Pr.23.20-21","rawR":"Pr.23.20-21","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Pr.23.20-21","value":"Pr.23.20-21"}],"t":"ref","v":"Prov 23:20-21"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Deut.21.20","refs":[{"book":"DEU","endChapter":21,"endVerse":20,"startChapter":21,"startVerse":20}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Deut.21.20","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"DEU","sourceChapterCoverage":[21],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"253deb2663cc6f76a48ce2b9f19046705fb712735e0aa97224b93b97c6d89c2d","sourceItemName":"Deut.21.20","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":2292,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"DEU","canonicalOSIS":"Deut","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Deut"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Deut.21.20","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Deut.21.20","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Deut.21.22","r":"Deut.21.22","rawR":"Deut.21.22","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.21.22","value":"Deut.21.22"}],"t":"ref","v":"21:22"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["hung on a tree:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," This was not the method of execution, at least in this text. It was a shameful display of those already put to death for capital offenses, probably to show the Lord’s hatred of sin and to deter others who might commit such acts (see also ",{"href":"?bref=Gen.40.19","r":"Gen.40.19","rawR":"Gen.40.19","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.40.19","value":"Gen.40.19"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gen 40:19"},", ",{"href":"?bref=Gen.40.22","r":"Gen.40.22","rawR":"Gen.40.22","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.40.22","value":"Gen.40.22"}],"t":"ref","v":"22"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Josh.10.26","r":"Josh.10.26","rawR":"Josh.10.26","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Josh.10.26","value":"Josh.10.26"}],"t":"ref","v":"Josh 10:26"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.4.12","r":"2Sam.4.12","rawR":"2Sam.4.12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.4.12","value":"2Sam.4.12"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Sam 4:12"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=2Sam.21.12","r":"2Sam.21.12","rawR":"2Sam.21.12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Sam.21.12","value":"2Sam.21.12"}],"t":"ref","v":"21:12"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Esth.2.23","r":"Esth.2.23","rawR":"Esth.2.23","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Esth.2.23","value":"Esth.2.23"}],"t":"ref","v":"Esth 2:23"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Esth.7.10","r":"Esth.7.10","rawR":"Esth.7.10","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Esth.7.10","value":"Esth.7.10"}],"t":"ref","v":"7:10"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Deut.21.22","refs":[{"book":"DEU","endChapter":21,"endVerse":22,"startChapter":21,"startVerse":22}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Deut.21.22","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"DEU","sourceChapterCoverage":[21],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"f70a7277466bd1ddeb58ed97af7aca39eee2b73aaa2d93f875afbc199f1a1986","sourceItemName":"Deut.21.22","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":2293,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"DEU","canonicalOSIS":"Deut","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Deut"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Deut.21.22","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Deut.21.22","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Deut.21.23","r":"Deut.21.23","rawR":"Deut.21.23","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.21.23","value":"Deut.21.23"}],"t":"ref","v":"21:23"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["for anyone who is hung:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," The Greek version reads ",{"c":["for everyone who is hung on a tree."],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," Cp. ",{"href":"?bref=Gal.3.13","r":"Gal.3.13","rawR":"Gal.3.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gal.3.13","value":"Gal.3.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gal 3:13"},", which explains how this proverb applies to Jesus. • ",{"c":["cursed in the sight of God:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," Unrepentant sinners do fall under God’s judgment. However, the idea of this proverb is probably that whoever saw a person in this condition would naturally conclude that God had cursed that person, when in fact the person could have been misjudged or even lynched."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Deut.21.23","refs":[{"book":"DEU","endChapter":21,"endVerse":23,"startChapter":21,"startVerse":23}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Deut.21.23","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"DEU","sourceChapterCoverage":[21],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"b78a51ce13a4eb5efb5705b2c2142ec04c3b1eda039642f0efbd81e79ec41db0","sourceItemName":"Deut.21.23","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":2294,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"DEU","canonicalOSIS":"Deut","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Deut"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Deut.21.23","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Deut.21.23","type":"StudyNote"}],"version":"v2-a101d843a2f0cd3fc0c6"}